

IFCONFIG(8)         UNIX Programmer's Manual         IFCONFIG(8)



NAME
     ifconfig - configure network interface parameters

SYNOPSIS
     ifconfig
     ifconfig -a
     ifconfig interface
     ifconfig interface inet address netmask mask broadcast addr

DESCRIPTION
     Ifconfig shows or configures a network interface.  With no
     arguments (or -a) it lists every interface; with an interface
     name it shows that one; with address arguments it assigns them.

     The status line reports the real interface flags read from the
     kernel: UP and RUNNING appear only when the card is present and
     the link is up (a card that is down no longer reads as up),
     BROADCAST for the Ethernet medium.  The options line lists the
     W5100's hardware offload -- IPv4, ARP, TCP, UDP, inbound ICMP,
     and the IP/TCP/UDP checksums are all computed by the card:

          u2s1: flags=67<UP,BROADCAST,RUNNING>
                  options=<TCP4,UDP4,ICMP4,ARP,CSUM_IP,CSUM_TCP,CSUM_UDP>
                  ether 00:08:dc:00:00:01
                  inet 192.168.2.10 netmask 0xffffff00 broadcast 192.168.2.255

     Interfaces are named u2sN for the Uthernet-II (W5100) card in
     slot N.  The address, netmask, and broadcast keywords may be
     abbreviated to their first letter.

     The v1 tool talks to the kernel ifnet table over SYS_IFCONFIG.
     The BSD socket-ioctl model (SIOCGIFCONF / SIOCGIFFLAGS) is a
     roadmap item; see the Track B netinet-port scope.

FILES
     /etc/hostname       interface name read at boot
     /etc/netstart       brings interfaces up at login

SEE ALSO
     dhclient(8), route(8), netstat(1)

BUGS
     Only one Uthernet-II card is driven; a second card is detected
     but not used (multi-NIC is a roadmap item).  Loopback (lo0) is
     not yet present -- it arrives with the software stack.



